WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.535
The server crashes mid-transaction.

00:00:05.535 --> 00:00:09.070
Power dies, a disk faults, the network drops.

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:12.606
On reboot the database has to answer one question.

00:00:12.606 --> 00:00:16.141
Which work survives, and which gets thrown away?

00:00:16.141 --> 00:00:19.676
Committed work survives.

00:00:19.676 --> 00:00:23.212
Once you saw COMMIT, that work is kept.

00:00:23.212 --> 00:00:26.747
Work that never committed is thrown away, reversed as if it never ran.

00:00:26.747 --> 00:00:30.282
The whole answer lives in the WAL, and recovery is the procedure that reads it.

00:00:30.282 --> 00:00:34.488
The whole algorithm is one WAL, read down then up.

00:00:34.488 --> 00:00:38.695
Here is the log, five transactions with their writes interleaved, and the start state.

00:00:38.695 --> 00:00:42.901
Value A is ten, B twenty, C thirty, D forty, E fifty.

00:00:42.901 --> 00:00:47.108
Then the crash.

00:00:47.108 --> 00:00:51.314
The disk holds whatever was written before it hit, not a consistent state.

00:00:51.314 --> 00:00:55.521
So first, the REPLAY pass runs down the log, re-applying every row, the original writes and the in-log abort rows alike.

00:00:55.521 --> 00:00:59.727
That rebuilds the exact crash state.

00:00:59.727 --> 00:01:03.934
Value A is twelve, B twenty, C forty-three point two, D forty-eight, E sixty.

00:01:03.934 --> 00:01:08.140
Now, which transactions does the UNDO pass reverse?

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:12.347
T four committed last of all, right before the crash.

00:01:12.347 --> 00:01:16.553
T three and T five.

00:01:16.553 --> 00:01:20.760
They never committed.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:24.966
T four committed late, so it survives.

00:01:24.966 --> 00:01:29.173
Then the UNDO pass runs back up, reversing only the uncommitted transactions, newest first.

00:01:29.173 --> 00:01:33.379
E goes sixty back to fifty.

00:01:33.379 --> 00:01:37.586
C goes forty-three point two back to thirty-six, then thirty-six back to thirty.

00:01:37.586 --> 00:01:41.792
What is left is the final consistent state.

00:01:41.792 --> 00:01:45.999
Value A twelve, B twenty, C thirty, D forty-eight, E fifty.

00:01:45.999 --> 00:01:50.205
T1 and T4 committed, so they are kept.

00:01:50.205 --> 00:01:54.412
T2 aborted, already rolled back in the log.

00:01:54.412 --> 00:01:58.618
T3 and T5 never committed, so undo reverses them.

00:01:58.618 --> 00:02:03.092
On reboot, RAM is gone, every in-progress change with it.

00:02:03.092 --> 00:02:07.565
The disk survived, but it is a half-written mix.

00:02:07.565 --> 00:02:12.039
A committed write is missing, and an uncommitted write is wrongly present.

00:02:12.039 --> 00:02:16.513
Only the WAL survived, untouched.

00:02:16.513 --> 00:02:20.986
So recovery fixes the disk from the WAL alone, not one byte from RAM.

00:02:20.986 --> 00:02:25.460
Replay redoes the missing committed writes, then undo reverses the wrong ones.

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:29.261
Two guarantees fall out of this.

00:02:29.261 --> 00:02:33.063
Durability comes from the replay.

00:02:33.063 --> 00:02:36.864
Once you see COMMIT, that work is replayed and never undone, so it survives any failure.

00:02:36.864 --> 00:02:40.666
Atomicity comes from the undo pass.

00:02:40.666 --> 00:02:44.467
A transaction is all or nothing, even across a crash, because unfinished work is reversed.

00:02:44.467 --> 00:02:48.269
The write-ahead rule makes both hold.

00:02:48.269 --> 00:02:52.070
The log reaches the disk before the data, so the log is always enough to finish the story.

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:58.200
One log, read down to replay and up to undo, turns a crash into a recoverable event.

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:04.330
That is the last piece of the transaction manager.

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:10.460
Together with scheduling, locking, and logging, it is what lets a database promise ACID while thousands of transactions run at once.
